  {
   "_id": 11422,
   "ANSWER": "<p data-end=\"2008\" data-start=\"1821\">In procedural programming, execution flows in a predefined sequence. In event-driven programming, control depends on events like mouse clicks or keypresses.</p>\n\n<p>from tkinter import *</p>\n\n<p>def on_click():<br />\n&nbsp; &nbsp; print(&quot;Button clicked&quot;)</p>\n\n<p>window = Tk()<br />\nButton(window, text=&quot;Click&quot;, command=on_click).pack()<br />\nwindow.mainloop()<br />\n&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p data-end=\"5328\" data-start=\"5130\">Here, <code data-end=\"5148\" data-start=\"5136\">on_click()</code> runs <strong data-end=\"5167\" data-start=\"5154\">only when</strong> the button is clicked, not sequentially. This model improves responsiveness and is suited for GUIs, games, or IoT systems where asynchronous behavior is needed.</p>\n",
   "COURSE_CODE": "21CSC203P",
   "LEVEL": 4,
   "OUTCOMES": [
    "PLO1",
    "PLO3",
    "PLO4",
    "PLO5",
    "PLO9"
   ],
   "QUESTION_DESC": "<p>Analyze how event-driven programming differs from procedural programming in terms of control flow and responsiveness.</p>\n",
